    "practice": [
      "Which statement concerns authentication rather than authorization?|Evidence of account control|Permission to edit a grade~That is a decision about an allowed business action.|Approval of a particular refund~Transaction approval is separate from identifying the caller.|A tenant-specific export grant~The grant defines what the principal may access.|Authentication supplies identity-related assurance; authorization decides whether that identity may perform an action.",
      "Do a password and a security question necessarily provide distinct factor categories?|No; both can be knowledge|Yes; there are two prompts~Prompt count does not establish different categories.|Yes; questions are possession~Knowing an answer does not demonstrate possession of a device.|Only if the answers are short~Answer length does not change the factor category.|Two knowledge checks do not become multi-factor authentication merely because the screens differ.",
      "What should be checked before claiming a passwordless method is phishing-resistant?|The actual protocol properties|Only the absence of a password box~Removing a field does not establish origin binding or replay resistance.|Only the method\u2019s marketing name~A label is not evidence of the implemented protocol.|Only the speed of the login animation~Display timing does not establish authentication assurance.|Attack resistance depends on the protocol, binding and implemented verification rather than a passwordless label.",
      "A student passes MFA and requests a grade edit. What remains necessary?|Grade-edit authorization|A second welcome screen~Presentation does not grant the operation.|Only a longer student username~Identifier length does not define permissions.|No check after MFA success~MFA does not turn every authenticated user into an administrator.|The application must evaluate whether the identified principal may perform the protected grade change.",
      "A service accepts a JSON field stating mfa=true from the browser. What is missing?|Trusted assurance evidence|A capitalized field name~Changing spelling does not establish trusted provenance.|A second copy of the same field~Duplicated untrusted claims remain untrusted.|A shorter request timeout~Timeouts do not validate an authentication assertion.|Authentication assurance must come from trusted validated evidence rather than caller-controlled flags.",
      "A strong login method is deployed while account recovery trusts an unverified email. Which path needs review?|The weaker recovery flow|Only the login button\u2019s size~Presentation does not address a recovery bypass.|Only the sign-in page title~A title cannot strengthen account recovery.|No path after passwordless enrollment~A weak alternate path can undermine the intended assurance.|An attacker may bypass strong primary authentication by exploiting a less trustworthy recovery mechanism."
    ],
    "formal": [
      "Which factor category ordinarily includes a memorized password?|Knowledge factor|Possession~Remembering a secret does not itself demonstrate control of a physical authenticator.|Biometric inherence~A password is not a biological characteristic.|Resource ownership~Owning a business resource is not a password factor category.|A memorized secret is a knowledge factor within the authentication model.",
      "What does reauthentication add before a sensitive operation?|Renewed account assurance|Universal action permission~Fresh authentication does not grant an otherwise forbidden action.|Automatic payment approval~An authenticated principal still needs transaction authorization.|Permanent device trust~Fresh proof does not make a device trustworthy forever.|Reauthentication can refresh evidence of account control, while permission remains a separate decision.",
      "Why is authenticator enrollment security-sensitive?|It binds future account access|It only chooses interface colors~Enrollment controls how an authenticator becomes associated with the identity.|It cannot affect later sign-ins~A maliciously bound authenticator may enable future access.|It is irrelevant after one login~The enrolled method can remain an ongoing access path.|Binding an authenticator determines which evidence the service may later accept for an account.",
      "A portal asks for a password twice and calls it MFA. Which assessment is accurate?|Both prompts use knowledge factors|The second prompt proves possession~Repeating a secret does not prove possession of an authenticator.|Two prompts guarantee phishing resistance~An attacker can collect repeated knowledge secrets too.|A duplicate password adds authorization~Authentication prompts do not grant business permissions.|The number of prompts is not the number of independent authentication-factor categories.",
      "An application receives a signed assurance assertion but does not check its intended audience. What risk remains?|Acceptance outside its context|Only the signature\u2019s text length~Length does not establish that this application is the intended relying party.|Only a missing display name~A name is not the audience-validation requirement.|The assertion must be a password~Assertions and passwords are different credential representations.|A valid signature alone does not establish that the evidence was issued for this relying party and context.",
      "A biometric unlocks a local security key. Which claim exceeds that observation?|The server stores the biometric|A local verification occurred~The described local unlock supports that limited observation.|The key can have recovery needs~Device loss remains a legitimate lifecycle concern.|The protocol still needs validation~Local unlocking does not remove protocol requirements.|A local biometric check does not imply that the server receives or stores the biometric template.",
      "A bank requires two categories and transaction permission. A caller supplies two passwords and has a valid role. Which result fits the stated policy?|Authentication requirement unmet|Role membership supplies a factor~A permission role is not a second authentication factor.|Two secrets always meet MFA~Both secrets can belong to the same knowledge category.|Permission alone approves the transfer~The policy independently requires the authentication condition.|A valid role does not compensate for missing distinct-category authentication evidence.",
      "A phishing-resistant authenticator is required for administrators, but device recovery creates a password-only administrator session. What should reviewers examine?|Whether recovery preserves assurance|Only the recovery email\u2019s formatting~Formatting does not establish equivalent authentication assurance.|Only the number of enrolled users~Enrollment counts do not test the alternate access path.|Whether the role name is uppercase~Role capitalization does not affect recovery protection.|An alternate recovery path can defeat the assurance expected for privileged accounts.",
      "A user loses a device and cannot use the preferred authenticator. Which design balances access and assurance?|An approved identity recovery process|An unverified support override~An easy override may admit an attacker claiming device loss.|A publicly shared backup account~Shared accounts weaken attribution and increase access exposure.|Disabling every future account check~A permanent bypass does not provide bounded recovery.|Recovery needs accessible, controlled identity verification and authenticator lifecycle handling.",
      "Policy requires a possession factor plus a knowledge factor and finance permission for a transfer. Evidence includes a verified device signature, a repeated password prompt and an expired finance grant. What follows?|Deny because the grant expired|Allow because two categories exist~Authentication requirements do not restore expired authorization.|Allow because passwords were repeated~Repeated prompts do not create an active finance permission.|Allow after renaming the role~A label change does not renew the expired grant.|Even if the authentication categories are satisfied, the independent current finance permission is missing."
    ]
